// Lander II - Williams: the sound board of the Williams arcade machines,
// recreated for the Arcade sound set.
//
// Credit: Williams Electronics Defender (1980) / Robotron: 2084 (1982)
// sound ROM, by Sam Dicker; routines and tables transcribed from the
// original source.
//
// Defender, Stargate and Robotron share one sound board. On it a little
// 6808 processor, clocked at 894,886 cycles a second, writes numbers from
// 0 to 255 to an 8-bit converter (a "DAC") that drives the loudspeaker.
// There is no sample rate and no sound chip: every sound is a loop of
// machine code whose instructions take a known number of cycles, so the
// length of a loop sets a pitch and the numbers it writes set the shape
// of the wave. The main game sends the board a command number (1 to 31 on
// Defender, 1 to 63 on Robotron); the board drops whatever it was playing
// on the spot and starts the new sound.
//
// This file does what the board did, cycle for cycle. Each routine below
// follows Sam Dicker's program instruction by instruction, counting the
// cycles each instruction takes (the numbers in the c(...) calls), and
// notes the value and the cycle of every write to the DAC. render() then
// turns that list of writes into samples: it averages the DAC level over
// each output sample (a "box filter"), removes the DC offset (the DAC
// only makes positive levels) and rounds off the top a little, as the
// amplifier and speaker in the cabinet did. The parameter tables are
// copied from the original source, so the sounds come out the same: the
// same pitches, the same noise (a 16-bit shift register, just as on the
// board) and the same odd little glitches that make them recognisable.
//
// The routines, in brief:
//   GWAVE   a wavetable player: a short wave (a sine, a square, ...)
//           played at a pattern of pitches, with "echoes" that decay the
//           wave by subtracting a fraction of it each time (with 8-bit
//           wrap-around, which gives the gritty edge), and a pitch offset
//           that can step up or down on every pass
//   VARI    a square wave whose two halves are timed separately, both
//           sweeping as it plays (the rising "whoop", the falling chirps)
//   LITEN   random toggles of the DAC with a sweeping delay (lightning,
//           and "appear" as a ship or alien materialises)
//   NOISE   random bits at a falling rate and level (Defender's laser)
//   FNOISE  "filtered" noise: the level slews towards random targets,
//           never faster than a set slope; the slope can shrink as it
//           plays (the explosion) or stay put (the thrust and the
//           background rumble)
//   RADIO, HYPER, SCREAM, ORGAN (tunes), SP1 (spinner), BON2 (a warble
//           rising a step each time it is sent), BG2 (the turbine)
//   Robotron adds SING (a square wave stepping in pitch: the siren),
//   ZIREN (the air-raid siren), WHIST (the falling bomb) and PLANE.
//
// How the game uses it (SfxArcade does this):
//
//   render('laser')              a named sound (see SOUNDS), as samples
//   render(0x17)                 a command number (Defender's board)
//   render(0x31, { rom: 'robotron', seconds: 1.2 })
//   render([[0, 0x11], [0.133, 0x11], [0.267, 0x17]])  a sequence: each
//                                command cuts off the one before
//   render([[0, 'fnoise', { fmax: 0xff, sampc: 150, fdflg: 1, dsflg: 1 }]])
//                                a routine with parameters of our own
//   renderLoop('THRUST', { seconds: 1.5 })   a buffer that loops cleanly
//
// render's options: rom ('defender' or 'robotron'), seconds (cut the
// sound off there, with a short fade), until (['note', 7]: cut where the
// 8th note starts; also 'gwave' passes, 'vari' sweeps, 'siren' cycles),
// fade (seconds), rate (samples a second, 48000), seed (varies the
// noise; the board's own noise never repeats), filter (false: the raw
// DAC levels, -1..1).
//
// Nothing here uses the DOM or Web Audio, so it runs under node too.

